lly network is available (\"network access is enabled\"). But for evaluation isolation, we're told to work only within the workspace using ./inputs files. We shouldn't fetch external data… \"use only files under ./inputs\". So no downloading public profiles. Evidence has to come from marker coherence and promoter accessibility using the provided GTF.\n\nPlan:\n1. Parse the GTF to get gene coordinates (gene-level: chrom, strand, TSS). Axolotl gene names in the GTF — let me check what attributes exist. Official axolotl annotation uses gene names like \"AMEXT_…\" or symbols? Let me check.\n\n2. Define marker gene lists for the 15 organs using axolotl gene symbols (human/mouse orthologs). Axolotl genome annotation: gene symbols in GTF may be things like \"LOC1030…\" or official symbols. Need to check.\n\n3. Compute per-sample promoter signal (e.g., counts within ±2kb of TSS) and gene-body signal for each marker set, and for each organ compute a \"coherence\" score: how well the sample labeled as that organ matches its marker set compared to other samples.\n\n4. For each unordered pair of organs (X,Y), compute a swap score: evidence that sample X contains organ Y's profile and sample Y contains organ X's profile. Score = e.g. (similarity(X-labeled sample → Y markers) - similarity(X-labeled sample → X markers)) + symmetric term. Rank pairs.\n\n5. Write output files.\n\nFirst, let me check the GTF format."}}
