{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"reasoning","text":"Key observations:\n1. There are 99,088 genes. GTF coordinates are in arm space: e.g., chr1p's maximum coordinate 1,417,632,714 ≈ sum of each piece of chr1p (472565920+475274080+469857632 = 1,417,697,632). Close value (slightly smaller than the total, consistent). chr14q maximum value 436,427,145 ≈ 436,437,773. Good — GTF is in concatenated arm space.\n\n2. There are many unplaced contigs ('C0000568', etc.). Genes on these contigs can't be mapped to the count table (the table only has chromosome arms). That's fine — use only genes on arms.\n\n3. gene_name format: \"SYMBOL [nr]|SYMBOL [hs]\" — the [hs] token is the human ortholog symbol. Some genes have only the AMEX ID (no homology). Also LOC... names with [nr] exist.\n\nNext, need to build a symbol→gene index from the [hs] tag (and possibly [nr] for non-human). Then define marker sets and check which are present.\n\nAnother important point: multiple genes can share the same [hs] symbol. For marker scoring, take all genes matching the marker symbol (or pick the most highly expressed/promoter-accessible one).\n\nNow, marker sets. Need to carefully pick robust markers per organ. Let me define candidate human gene symbols per organ (about 8–15 each) and check which are present in the [hs] index:\n\n- Bladder: UPK1A, UPK1B, UPK2, UPK3A, UPK3B, KRT20, KRT7, PPARG, FABP4? (urothelium: UPK2, UPK3A are canonical), SLC26A6? Also CHRM? I'll use UPK1A, UPK1B, UPK2, UPK3A, UPK3B, KRT7, KRT20, ETV4? No. Also urothelial plakoglobin? Keep: UPK1A, UPK1B, UPK2, UPK3A, UPK3B, KRT20, KRT7.\n- Brain: SNAP25, SYT1, SYN1, SYP, NCAM1, MAP2, TUBB3, NEFL, NEFM, NEFH, ENO2, GAD1, GAD2, GRIN1, GRIN2B, GFAP, MBP, MOG, PLP1, OLIG1? Use a mix of neuronal: SNAP25, SYT1, SYN1, SYP, NCAM1, MAP2, TUBB3, NEFL, NEFM, NEFH, ENO2, GAD1, GRIN2B, KCNJ10? And glial GFAP, MBP, PLP1, MOG.\n- Cloaca: difficult; cloaca = common chamber of hindgut + urogenital sinus. Markers might be mixed: intestinal (CDX2?) and urothelial. Honestly no good canonical marker set. Might exclude from marker scoring or use weak markers. But if the swapped pair involves Cloaca, marker coherence alone can't resolve it… Let me see the results first. Maybe use HOXC cluster? Cloaca development: HOXC9-13? Hmm.\n- GallBladder: KRT7, KRT19, KRT8, KRT18, SOX9, EPCAM, MUC1, ANXA4, SLC4A2, SLC26A6 (SLC26A6 is gallbladder/bicarbonate), CFTR? Also ONECUT1 (HNF6, biliary), HNF1B? Biliary epithelium: ONECUT1, HNF1B, SOX9, KRT19. Gallbladder-specific: SLC26A6, ANXA4, MUC5AC? I'll use: KRT7, KRT19, KRT8, KRT18, SOX9, EPCAM, ANXA4, SLC26A6, ONECUT1, HNF1B.\n- Gill: RHBG, RHCG, ATP1A1, ATP1B1, CA2, CA4, CA12? SLC4A4, SLC4A1? SLC12A2 (NKCC1), SLC9A3, SLC26A6? Keratin? Gill ionocyte markers in fish: ATP1A1, CA2/CA4, RHBG/RHCG, SLC4A1? Also SLC26A3? Use: RHBG, RHCG, ATP1A1, ATP1B3? CA2, CA4, SLC4A4, SLC12A2, SLC9A3.\n- Heart: MYH6, MYH7, TNNT2, TNNI3, TNNI1?, ACTC1, MYL2, MYL3, MYL4, MYL7, NPPA, NPPB, RYR2, ATP2A2, KCNJ2, GJA1, GJA5, TBX5, GATA4, MEF2C, HAND2, ACTN2, DES.\n- Intestine: VIL1, CDX2, FABP1, FABP2, FABP6, SI, LCT, DPP4, MUC2, APOA1? (also liver), APOA4, APOB (also liver), REG4, TREH, SLC5A1, SLC15A1, GUCA2A, GUCA2B, CDX1, HNF4A? (also liver). Use: VIL1, CDX2, FABP2, SI, LCT, DPP4, MUC2, REG4, TREH, SLC5A1, GUCA2A, APOA4.\n- Kidney: AQP1, AQP2, SLC12A1 (NKCC2, TAL), SLC12A3 (NCC, DCT), UMOD, CALB1, SLC8A1, NPHS1, NPHS2, PODXL, WT1, LRP2 (megalin), CUBN, SLC22A6 (OAT1), SLC22A8, GGT1, KCNJ1, CLCNKB? Also PAX8, HNF1B. Use: AQP1, AQP2, SLC12A1, SLC12A3, UMOD, CALB1, NPHS1, NPHS2, PODXL, LRP2, CUBN, SLC22A6, GGT1, PAX8.\n- Limb: HOXA9, HOXA10, HOXA11, HOXA13, HOXD9, HOXD10, HOXD11, HOXD12, HOXD13, MEIS1, MEIS2, TBX5, TBX4, PITX1, MSX1, MSX2, DLX5, DLX6? GREM1, FGF8? SHH? Use HOX + MEIS + TBX5/4 + MSX1/2 + DLX5 + PITX1 + GREM1.\n- Liver: ALB, AFP, APOA1, APOA2, APOB, APOC2, APOC3, APOE, SERPINA1, SERPINA3? AGT, TTR, FGA, FGB, FGG, HP, HPR? CRP? CYP1A2, CYP2E1, CYP3A4, HNF4A, HNF1A, ONECUT1, CETP? Also FGL1, LECT2. Use: ALB, AFP, APOA1, APOA2, APOB, APOC3, SERPINA1, AGT, TTR, FGA, FGB, FGG, HP, HNF4A, CYP3A4? (maybe absent), FGL1.\n- Lung: SFTPA1, SFTPA2, SFTPB, SFTPC, SFTPD, ABCA3, NKX2-1, SCGB1A1, SCGB3A1, MUC1? AQP5, FXYD3? Also HOPX (AT1), AKAP5? AT2: SFTPC, ABCA3, LAMP3? Use: SFTPA1, SFTPB, SFTPC, SFTPD, ABCA3, NKX2-1, SCGB1A1, AQP5, HOPX, SCGB3A2? Note: axolotl lungs are simple sacs; surfactant proteins should be there.\n- Pancreas: INS, GCG, SST, PPY, PDX1, MAFA, MAFB, NKX6-1, NKX2-2, ISL1, SLC30A8, IAPP, PCSK1, PCSK2, REG1A, REG1B, CELA3A, CELA2A, CPA1, CPA2, CPB1, CTRB1, PRSS1, PRSS2, GDF15? Exocrine + endocrine.\n- Prostate: KLK3, KLK2, NKX3-1, HOXB13, TMPRSS2, ACPP, MSMB, STEAP4? STEAP1? AR? Also KLK4? SLC45A3? Use: KLK3, NKX3-1, HOXB13, TMPRSS2, ACPP, MSMB, AR. Caveat: does axolotl have a prostate? There's a \"prostate\" sample in this dataset (probably from a published axolotl ATAC atlas). Hmm, actually I recall a 2024/2025 axolotl organ ATAC-seq atlas paper… maybe \"A single-cell atlas of axolotl…\" but anyway, the dataset exists.\n- Spleen: hematopoietic/immune: HBA1? Amphibian erythrocytes are nucleated → globin loci are open: HBA, HBB. Also immune: CD3E, CD3D, CD8A, CD4? CD79A, CD19, MS4A1? SPI1, LYZ, LYZ2? MPO? ELANE? ITGAM, ITGAX, CSF1R, PTPRC (CD45), RAG1, RAG2? Terminal deoxynucleotidyl? Use: HBB (HBA/HBB family), PTPRC, CD3E, CD79A, SPI1, LYZ, CSF1R, ITGAM, RAG1, MPO, GATA1? TFRC? Erythroid: ALAS2, TFRC, GYPA? Spleen = mixture of blood cells.\n- Stomach: GHRL, PGA3, PGA4, PGA5, PGC, ATP4A, ATP4B, MUC5AC, MUC6, GKN1, GKN2? CHIA? LIPF? Also GAST? (gastrin — expressed in G cells), HDC? Use: GHRL, PGA3, PGC, ATP4A, ATP4B, MUC5AC, MUC6, GKN1, LIPF, GAST, HDC.\n\nNote: amphibian stomach pepsinogen genes might be annotated as PGA/PGC. Ghrelin (GHRL) is a good marker.\n\nThe [hs] tag is a human symbol; not all genes will have a human ortholog assigned. Presence check needed. Also note: gene_name might contain symbols like \"NKX2-1 [hs]\" — the dash needs exact matching.\n\nNext, the scoring approach. Let me think carefully about how to make it robust:\n\nFor each gene g, promoter window: TSS ± 2kb (or [-1500, +500]?). ATAC-seq promoter peaks are centered on TSS. Use TSS ± 2kb and sum counts of overlapping bins. Counts are per 10kb bin, so ±2kb captures partial bins. Simplest and most robust: take bins overlapping [TSS-2000, TSS+2000]. With 10kb bins, usually 1 bin (2 bins if near boundary). Also normalize by library size per sample (counts per million). Also, to control for local background/accessibility variance, subtract a local control: e.g., signal in bins ±20-50kb? That adds complexity; maybe use fold-change over chromosome-arm median? Hmm.\n\nBetter normalization: for each sample s, define lib_s = column total. Signal = raw_count / lib_s * 1e6. Then for each gene and sample we get a promoter CPM value. For each organ marker set M_O (a list of genes), sample score: for each gene, rank or z-score across samples, then average rank across marker genes; or mean CPM of marker genes normalized per gene by its mean across samples. The per-gene z-score across 15 samples is clean: z_s = (x_s - mean)/std; organ score for sample s = mean of z across marker genes. This controls for expression level differences between genes.\n\nThen:\n- coherence(O) = z-score of the labeled sample vs. the scores of all samples for marker set O: specifically, rank of sample O among the 15 samples on its own markers; ideally rank 1.\n- mismatch(O → best) = which sample has the highest score for organ O's markers.\n\nSwap detection: a swap between organs A and B means the sample labeled A contains organ B's data and vice versa. Evidence:\n1. Sample A scores high on B's markers (z_A(M_B) high, ideally rank 1 among samples on M_B) and sample B scores high on A's markers.\n2. Sample A scores low on A's own markers (some other sample has A's true profile — but if the swap is complete, the true organ A's profile is in column B).\n\nSo define for ordered pair (A,B): e(A→B) = z_A(M_B) - z_A(M_A), where z_A(M_O) = sample A's z-score for marker set O (gene-level z averaged across genes). If labels are swapped between A and B: column A contains B tissue → z_A(M_B) should be strongly positive (B tissue is the top sample for B markers — assuming B's own markers rank samples with B tissue at top… caveat: if the label swap confuses the z-scores… z is computed across the 15 columns, which is fine).\n\nswap_score(A,B) = [z_A(M_B) + z_B(M_A)] - [z_A(M_A) + z_B(M_B)]… or simply e(A→B)+e(B→A). Also require z_A(M_B) ≥ z_B(M_B) (column A matches B's markers at least as well as column B does) and symmetrically.\n\nBut subtle: if A and B are swapped, then z_A(M_B) should be ≈ max_s z_s(M_B) and z_B(M_A) ≈ max_s z_s(M_A), and the true organ A sample (column B) will still score high on M_A… wait, if A↔B is fully swapped: column A = B tissue, column B = A tissue. Then:\n- M_A markers: best sample should be column B (true A tissue). z_B(M_A) = +high, z_A(M_A) = low.\n- M_B markers: best sample is column A. z_A(M_B) = +high, z_B(M_B) = low.\nSo e(A→B) = z_A(M_B) - z_A(M_A) >> 0 and e(B→A) >> 0.\n\nFor non-swap pairs: generally both terms ≤ 0 or small.\n\nThen rank all 105 unordered pairs by swap_score. If the swap is unique, rank 1 should clearly separate.\n\nAdditional evidence lines (following REFERENCE_NOTES, evidence_type can combine):\n(a) Promoter accessibility at marker genes (primary).\n(b) Organ marker coherence: for each organ, the \"expected best sample\" vs. the observed best sample.\n(c) Global profile similarity: e.g., for the candidate pair, verify that column A correlates with expected… hmm, no replicate. But another check: for each pair, we can compute similarity using genome-wide bins and public-like profiles: not available. Instead, use the second independent marker strategy: rank-based vs. z-based, or different promoter windows (±1kb, gene body). Or use tissue-specificity of top marker genes (single-gene checks, e.g., INS in pancreas).\n\nAlso, for the top candidate pair, do sanity checks on individual hallmark genes: e.g., if Brain↔X is suspected, verify SNAP25/SYT1 promoter signal is high in column X, etc.\n\nAdditionally, can use gene body+promoter \"activity\" and known organ identity of chromatin at specific loci: e.g., globin loci for spleen.\n\nAnother approach as robustness: unsupervised clustering of samples by marker-based scores and checking consistency.\n\nNow, the swapped pair might involve organs with weak markers (Cloaca, Gill, GallBladder, Prostate). Marker sets need decent coverage. Let me first check which [hs] symbols exist in the annotation.\n\nLet me build the symbol index and check presence for all candidate markers.\n\nAlso: some genes might have [hs] symbols but the annotation might use LOC names with the human symbol only in the [nr] token? [nr] token seems to be the best non-human hit (e.g., Xenopus 'ZFP37'). Primarily match [hs] and secondarily use [nr] for genes lacking [hs]. Hmm — risk: [nr] symbol could be a different species's paralog. Use [hs] as primary; if a marker is not found by [hs], check [nr].\n\nAnother consideration: multiple genes per symbol. E.g., ZNF268 appeared twice. For markers like INS there might be one. Take all matches but weight each gene equally in the z-average; duplicates slightly over-represent but tolerable. Better: dedupe — if multiple genes share the same [hs] symbol, keep them all but maybe cap. Let me look at the data first.\n\nNow, let me write an index-building script that creates a dictionary symbol → list of (arm, strand, tss). And check the markers.\n"}}
