{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"reasoning","text":"Marker coverage is uneven, but most organs have decent marker sets. Some gaps:\n- Pancreas: INS, GCG are missing! These are the most characteristic. Hmm. INS not found under [hs]. Let me search the GTF for 'INS' more loosely — INS might be annotated as 'INS [hs]', but my regex should have caught it... 'INS' is a substring of many words, but my matching is on whole tokens after splitting by '|'. Let me grep gene_name for 'INS' in the GTF to confirm. Also the 'GCG' token. Maybe the axolotl annotation has 'ins' as 'INS-gene'? Let me grep.\n- Lung: SFTPB/SFTPC are missing. Grep for 'SFTP'.\n- Heart: TNNT2 is missing. Grep for 'TNNT'.\n- Liver: AFP, APOB, AGT, FGB are missing. Grep for 'APOB', 'AFP'.\n- HOX genes are missing (HOXA/D clusters)? Strange — HOX genes definitely exist in axolotl. Maybe they're annotated as 'HOXA11 [nr]'? HOXC10 was found under [nr], HOXC13 under [hs]. Let me grep 'HOX' inside gene_name.\n- Spleen: HBB is missing. Grep for 'HBB', 'HBA', 'globin'.\n- Kidney: AQP1/AQP2, SLC12A1 are missing. Grep for 'AQP', 'SLC12'.\n- Prostate: KLK3 is missing; grep for 'KLK'.\n\nGene symbols in the annotation might use different naming conventions for some (e.g., 'itga', lowercase, or 'LOC...' without [hs] tag). Let me grep the full set of gene_name tokens to check for variants. Let me dump all gene_name strings and search them.\n\nAlso, note that UMOD had 24 hits — probably a large gene family (many genes with the UMOD [hs] tag, e.g., UMODL1? No, an exact-match token 'UMOD [hs]' appearing 24 times is weird — perhaps many axolotl genes have a best human hit of UMOD). This is the many-to-many problem; to avoid noise, I should limit marker genes to a small number of hits (e.g., ≤4) or pick the top ones. Actually, for scoring, having many genes per marker is OK if they're real paralogs, but for example 'MUC5AC [hs]' with 15 hits is suspicious (many mucin-like genes). Let me investigate these cases and curate: for symbols with many hits, pick based on synteny? That's too complex. Alternative: for symbols with many hits, keep the top 3 by… We can't know a priori. Perhaps keep all — z-scoring per gene and then averaging is relatively robust. Hmm, but 24 UMODs will dominate the kidney score. Let me cap at, say, the first 4 hits? Arbitrary but practical. Or filter: only keep hits whose [nr] token also matches the marker (if present)? Example: 'UMOD [nr]|UMOD [hs]'? Let me look at the actual gene_name strings for these symbols and curate manually.\n\nLet me grep gene names to investigate."}}
