{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting findings:\n- Some gene_name strings have no \"[hs]\"/\"[nr]\" tag at all: e.g., \"INS\" (AMEX60DD004533, AMEX60DD004535), \"GCG\", \"SFTPC\", \"TNNT2\", \"HOXA13\", \"HOXD10/11/13\", \"HOXB13\", \"SLC12A1\", \"APOB\", \"NKX3-1\", \"NKX2-1\", \"NKX6-1\", \"NKX2-2\". These are plain symbols without tags! My token regex `^(.*)\\s+\\[(\\w+)\\]$` requires a bracket suffix, so plain symbols were missed. Therefore my hs/nr index is incomplete: I should also index tokens that have no bracket suffix (treat as symbol, source 'plain').\n\n- INS: AMEX60DD004533 and 004535 on chr11q are plain \"INS\" — two insulin genes (axolotl has duplicated insulins). Also \"INS-IGF2 [hs]\" readthrough.\n- GCG: chr9q plain \"GCG\".\n- SFTPC plain. TNNT2 plain. HOXA13 plain. HOXD10/11/13 plain. HOXB13 plain. NKX3-1 plain. NKX2-1 plain. NKX6-1, NKX2-2 plain. SLC12A1 plain. APOB plain ×3 (three apolipoprotein B genes).\n- AFP: not found. OK.\n- AQP2 not found; AQP1 also not found? Only AQP11/12/10 hits. Amphibian aquaporins might have diverged names. Skip.\n- HBB not found as plain; HBA1 [hs] found. Amphibian globins may have diverged names. Search for 'HB' pattern? Amphibian globins in axolotl may be annotated as \"HBAD\"/\"HBB\" family… HBA1 [hs] was found on chr2p. Also search for 'HBM', 'HBE', 'HBZ'? Probably few. Keep HBA1, HBG1, ALAS2 for the erythroid lineage.\n\nSo: rebuild the symbol index including plain tokens. Then recheck marker presence.\n\nAlso, UMOD with 24 hits and MUC5AC with 15 hits, CHIA with 8 hits: many are probably gene family members tagged with these symbols as best hits. For robustness, for symbols with more than 5 hits, keep only the top hits by some criterion… which criterion? Option: use only genes where the symbol appears as a plain token or [nr] (ortholog assignment) vs [hs]? Hmm, the tag probably indicates the origin of the annotation transfer. Actually, more simply: for marker scoring, include all hits, but if a symbol has more than 6 hits, exclude that symbol entirely from the marker set (ambiguous assignment). E.g., UMOD 24 hits → exclude; MUC5AC 15 hits → exclude; CHIA 8 hits → exclude; SCN2A 9 hits → exclude; SLC12A2 6 hits → borderline. This keeps marker sets conservative.\n\nWait, UMOD is actually important as a kidney marker. Hmm. But ambiguous assignment adds noise; better to rely on other kidney markers (SLC12A3, NPHS1, LRP2, CUBN, SLC22A6, GGT1, CLCNKB, HNF1B, CALB1, PAX8?). PAX8 is missing. OK.\n\nSo now I'll rebuild the index including plain tokens, recheck, then finalize the marker sets.\n\nAlso, for genes found as plain tokens, they are probably curated orthologs (ambMex60DD annotation assigns plain symbols to 1:1 orthologs?).\n\nAdditionally, some markers were found via [nr] (e.g., TBX5, ENO2, CALB1, NPHS1, ALB, TTR). [nr] = non-redundant best hit (may be Xenopus symbol). These are usable with somewhat lower confidence.\n\nNext, let's finalize the marker sets (curated, plain tokens included):\n\nAfter rebuilding the index, recheck all candidate symbols and build the final marker dictionary in the main analysis script. Also add additional candidate markers I noticed might exist:\n- Heart: TNNT2 (plain), MYL7? Also search 'NPPA', 'MYL2', 'MYL7', 'TNNI3', 'GATA4'.\n- Pancreas: INS plain ×2, GCG plain. Also search 'IAPP', 'SLC30A8'.\n- Lung: SFTPC plain, SFTPD, NKX2-1 plain.\n- Spleen: search 'SPI1', 'LYZ', 'CD79', 'CD19', 'PTPRC'.\n- Liver: ALB, APOB plain ×3, FGA. Also search 'AGT', 'SERPINA'.\n- Prostate: NKX3-1 plain, HOXB13 plain, TMPRSS2, MSMB, KLK1.\n- Limb: HOXA13 plain, HOXD10/11/13 plain, MEIS1, MSX2, TBX5.\n- Bladder: few… also try 'UPK' (UPK3B was found). Also 'KRT20'? Not found. Bladder markers remain weak: UPK3B, KRT8/18, SLC26A6, maybe PPARG missing. Hmm, also 'FGFR3'? Urothelial differentiation factors: PPARG, ELF3, GRHL3? Check ELF3, GRHL.\n- Gill: good set.\n- Cloaca: weak (CDX2, HOXC13, WNT5A, TP63, HOXC10). Hmm.\n\nGiven the task, the swapped pair is probably identifiable via strong marker mismatch; even if one organ of the pair has weak markers, the swap score requires evidence in both directions. If the true swap involves Cloaca/Bladder/Prostate/GallBladder, evidence may be partial. The instructions say: \"If the evidence does not support a unique swap, return swap_detected=false and explain the uncertainty.\" But this benchmark probably has a designed swap. Let's look at the data.\n\nOK, rebuild the index including plain tokens, then compute promoter scores.\n\nPromoter window: TSS ±2kb. Bins are 10kb, so typically ±2kb falls within 1 bin; if TSS is within 2kb of a bin boundary, it overlaps two bins. Also add a broader window ±5kb? Let's use TSS ±2kb primarily and use gene body as auxiliary (ATAC signal in the gene body is correlated with transcription… for large genes, average coverage over gene body bins).\n\nNormalization: per-sample library size (column total) → CPM. Then z-score per gene across samples.\n\nLocal background correction: maybe subtract the median of bins ±50kb around TSS? That could sharpen the signal, but promoter ATAC peaks in a 10kb bin versus the background of neighboring bins: fold-change = promoter_bin / median(neighbors within ±100kb). This controls for large-scale accessibility domain differences. Hmm, this requires fetching neighboring bins — doable in the same streaming pass: for each gene, collect bins in [TSS-100kb, TSS+100kb], and define promoter bins (overlap with TSS ±2kb) and flank bins (the rest). Score = promoter_sum / (median(flank) + eps). But if a gene is inside a highly accessible domain, fold-change could be misleading. I'll compute both:\n1. Simple CPM z-score of the promoter bin.\n2. Fold-change over local flanking region.\nThen combine/verify.\n\nActually, for robustness of the final call, use two independent metrics and require agreement. Let's implement:\n\nPass 1: stream the TSV; for each gene on an arm, need bins overlapping gene region ±100kb. 99k genes × window — but we only need marker genes (~150-200 loci). So: build target intervals only for marker genes. For each arm, collect target bin ranges; stream the file once; for each row matching a target arm, if the row's start falls within a target interval, record the count.\n\nThen compute:\n- For each gene g, sample s: promoter count p_gs (sum of bins overlapping [tss-2000, tss+2000]) and flank count f_gs (sum of bins in [tss-100000, tss+100000] excluding promoter bins).\n- CPM: divide by column total ×1e6.\n- Fold: fc_gs = p_gs / (f_gs/n_flank_bins + eps)? Normalize per bin: promoter_per_bin / flank_per_bin.\n\nThen z-score across samples per gene.\n\nPer-organ marker score: average z across marker genes (each gene weighted equally).\n\nThen:\n- For each organ O: best_sample(O) = argmax_s score(s, M_O); coherence: rank of labeled sample O among samples for its own marker set.\n- Pairwise swap score: as defined earlier: e(A→B) = z_A(M_B) - z_A(M_A); swap_score(A,B) = e(A→B) + e(B→A). Also require both e terms to be >0 for \"supported\".\n- Additionally a \"consistency\" check: best_sample(A) == B and best_sample(B) == A.\n\nRank all 105 pairs by swap_score in descending order.\n\nOutput CSV format: one row per unordered organ pair, columns organ_a, organ_b, swap_score, rank, evidence_type. Names case-sensitive, lexicographically ordered within a row. rank=1 is the strongest. Score must be finite; higher = stronger evidence.\n\nFor evidence_type, use a combined string like \"promoter_accessibility+marker_coherence\" — the notes say \"Evidence may combine promoter accessibility, organ marker coherence, and comparison with public source profiles.\" Use descriptive tokens.\n\nNow, about \"comparison with public source profiles\": can't fetch public data (isolation rule). But there's an internal analogue: the marker coherence test is essentially a comparison with published marker knowledge. Note this in the report.\n\nAlso, as an additional robustness layer, compute a genome-wide correlation matrix between samples (15×15 Pearson over 10 kb bins, after log/CPM transform) and note clusters of related organs. If a sample's label is swapped, its global correlation pattern shifts to match its true tissue of origin… but without a reference for the true profile, correlation alone can't identify the swap; it just shows e.g. Bladder-Cloaca similarity. Actually wait — can we detect a swap via global correlations? If labels A and B are swapped, columns A and B still contain plausible tissue profiles (they just belong to different organs). Global correlations don't reveal label errors. So markers are the key.\n\nAnother orthogonal check: per-sample distribution statistics (fraction of reads in promoter regions, etc.) — weak evidence, skip.\n\nNow, a potential trap: if the swapped organ is one where markers are absent/weak (e.g., Pancreas INS missing), we need a fallback. INS is present as a plain token, good.\n\nAlso consider the possibility that a swap involves an organ whose marker set partially overlaps with another organ (e.g., Liver-GallBladder share biliary markers KRT8/18/19, ONECUT1, HNF1B; Intestine-Cloaca share CDX2; Bladder-Prostate share urothelial markers). For pairs with similar organs, scores may be ambiguous; require clear margins.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Load counts only for target bins: build a per-arm dictionary: arm → list of (bin_start, bin_end, row_index)? Actually more simply: during streaming, for each arm maintain a sorted list of target intervals and use a pointer; but rows are sorted by arm then by start; pieces are concatenated in arm coordinates; convert piece-local start to arm coordinates: arm_pos = piece_offset[piece] + start.\n- Need piece offsets per piece from chrom.sizes: pieces of an arm are concatenated in the order given in chrom.sizes.\n- GTF arm coordinates are 1-based; bin [start, end) is 0-based half-open. Promoter window [tss-2000, tss+2000] in arm coordinates; a bin overlaps if bin_arm_start < tss+2000 and bin_arm_end > tss-2000, where bin_arm_start = piece_offset + bin_start (bin_start is piece-local).\n\nLet me write the main analysis script now. Steps:\n1. Parse chrom.sizes → piece offsets per arm.\n2. Load genes (pickle), build symbol index (hs + nr + plain tokens).\n3. Define final marker sets with curated symbols (auto-filter: only symbols with 1..6 gene hits on arms; exclude contig-only genes).\n4. Build target intervals: for each marker gene: window [tss-100000, tss+100000]; store per-arm targets: (win_start, win_end, gene_idx, promoter_start, promoter_end).\n5. Stream TSV, accumulate counts per (gene, sample): promoter_counts[g][s], flank_counts[g][s], flank_nbins[g].\n6. Compute column totals (also accumulate during streaming — just sum every row? That requires summing 2.43M rows × 15 integers = fine, cheap).\n7. Compute scores, swap matrix, ranks; write outputs.\n\nMemory/time: streaming 2.43M rows with per-row work: parse 18 fields, check arm membership. ~5-15 seconds in pure Python; acceptable. Use sys.stdin-style iteration, split('\\t').\n\nColumn totals: compute from the same pass (add each row's values). 2.43M × 15 additions = 36M ops, OK in Python (~20-30 sec). Fine.\n\nMarker selection details: include both [hs] and plain tokens; use [nr] only if not found in hs/plain. If a symbol has >6 hits on arms → exclude as ambiguous (log it). Also exclude genes on unplaced contigs (can't be scored).\n\nLet me define the final marker sets (extended):\n\nBladder: UPK1A, UPK1B, UPK2, UPK3A, UPK3B, KRT20, KRT8, KRT18, PPARG, SLC26A6, PLAC8, ELF3, GRHL3, FGFR3, UPK? Also 'UPII'? Try 'SLC26A6'? Shared with gallbladder/gill/intestine — hmm, SLC26A6 is shared; not bladder-specific, but oh well, it's part of the signature set. Actually, to reduce cross-organ confusion, maybe drop markers that are shared across organs from the set? The scoring uses the full set; shared markers dilute specificity but add robustness. Keep them.\n\nBrain: SNAP25, SYT1, SYN1, SYP, NCAM1, MAP2, TUBB3, NEFL, NEFM, NEFH, ENO2, GAD1, GAD2, GRIN1, GRIN2B, GFAP, MBP, PLP1, MOG, OLIG2, SCN2A, KCNA2, SYT? Also 'GAP43'? 'DLG4' (PSD95), 'CAMK2A', 'GRIA1', 'GRIN2A'? Add DLG4, CAMK2A, GRIA2? Check availability later.\n\nCloaca: CDX2, HOXC10, HOXC11, HOXC12, HOXC13, WNT5A, TP63, KRT? 'GATA3'? (urogenital sinus), 'MSX2'? Hmm. Also 'SOX2' (cloacal epithelium?) Hmm. Keep the small set.\n\nGallBladder: KRT7, KRT19, KRT8, KRT18, SOX9, EPCAM, ANXA4, SLC26A6, ONECUT1, HNF1B, MUC1, CFTR, SLC4A2, ABCB11? (bile salt export — hepatocyte canalicular, actually), ABCB4? Also 'SLC10A2'? (ileum). 'SLC22A'? Keep: KRT7, KRT19, KRT8, KRT18, EPCAM, ANXA4, SLC26A6, ONECUT1, HNF1B, CFTR, SOX9, MUC1.\n\nGill: RHBG, RHCG, ATP1A1, ATP1B1, ATP6V1A, CA2, CA4, CA12, SLC4A4, SLC4A1, SLC12A2, SLC9A3, SLC26A6, SLC26A3, AQP3, also 'SLC26A4'? 'ATP6V0A'? Keep the list.\n\nHeart: MYH6, MYH7, TNNT2, TNNI3, ACTC1, MYL2, MYL3, MYL4, MYL7, NPPA, NPPB, RYR2, ATP2A2, KCNJ2, GJA1, GJA5, TBX5, GATA4, MEF2C, HAND2, ACTN2, DES, CASQ2, SCN5A, HCN4? Add if found.\n\nIntestine: VIL1, CDX2, FABP1, FABP2, FABP6, SI, LCT, DPP4, MUC2, REG4, TREH, SLC5A1, GUCA2A, APOA4, CDX1, SLC15A1, KRT20, REG3A? 'FABP5'? Keep the list.\n\nKidney: AQP1, AQP2, SLC12A1, SLC12A3, UMOD, CALB1, NPHS1, NPHS2, PODXL, LRP2, CUBN, SLC22A6, SLC22A8, GGT1, PAX8, WT1, KCNJ1, CLCNKB, HNF1B, also 'SLC34A1'? 'GATA3'? Keep.\n\nLimb: HOXA9, HOXA10, HOXA11, HOXA13, HOXD9, HOXD10, HOXD11, HOXD12, HOXD13, MEIS1, MEIS2, TBX5, TBX4, PITX1, MSX1, MSX2, DLX5, GREM1, FGF8, SHH, PBX1, HAND2, HOXC11? 'HOXC13'? Keep.\n\nLiver: ALB, AFP, APOA1, APOA2, APOB, APOC2, APOC3, APOE, SERPINA1, AGT, TTR, FGA, FGB, FGG, HP, HNF4A, HNF1A, ONECUT1, FGL1, CYP3A4, CYP2E1, SERPINC1, PROC, also 'LECT2', 'CRP', 'ORM1', 'AHSG', 'KNG1', 'AMBP', 'C9'? Add a few.\n\nLung: SFTPA1, SFTPA2, SFTPB, SFTPC, SFTPD, ABCA3, NKX2-1, SCGB1A1, SCGB3A1, SCGB3A2, AQP5, HOPX, MUC1, SLC34A2, FXYD3, LAMP3, also 'SLC6A14'? 'AGER'? Keep.\n\nPancreas: INS, GCG, SST, PPY, PDX1, MAFA, MAFB, NKX6-1, NKX2-2, ISL1, SLC30A8, IAPP, PCSK1, PCSK2, REG1A, REG1B, CELA3A, CELA2A, CPA1, CPA2, CPB1, CTRB1, PRSS1, PRSS2, also 'PNLIP', 'CEL', 'LIPA'? Keep.\n\nProstate: KLK3, KLK2, KLK4, NKX3-1, HOXB13, TMPRSS2, ACPP, MSMB, AR, STEAP4, SLC45A3, KLK1, also 'KLK7'? 'AZGP1'? Keep.\n\nSpleen: HBA1, HBA2, HBB, HBG1, ALAS2, TFRC, GYPA, PTPRC, CD3E, CD3D, CD79A, CD19, SPI1, LYZ, MPO, ELANE, CSF1R, ITGAM, ITGAX, RAG1, RAG2, GATA1, also 'LYZ2'? 'C1QA'? 'C1QB'? 'CD74A'? 'HLA-DRA'? 'FCER1G'? 'TYROBP'? Add a few immune: C1QA, CD74, HLA-DRA, FCGR? Keep the list and use whatever is found.\n\nStomach: GHRL, PGA3, PGA4, PGA5, PGC, ATP4A, ATP4B, MUC5AC, MUC6, GKN1, LIPF, GAST, HDC, CHIA, MUC1, TFF1, TFF2, also 'GIF' (intrinsic factor)? 'CLIC6'? Keep.\n\nThe auto-filter handles presence/absence; if fewer than ~4 markers are found per organ, treat that organ's marker set as weak (note in evidence).\n\nNow, write the main analysis script. Put it in scratch/ first for development, then polish into output/analysis.py (deliverable) that reproduces everything. Actually, better: write output/analysis.py as the definitive script and run it. Create output/ directory now.\n\nDeliverable script design: self-contained, reads inputs/, writes output/swap_call.json, output/sample_similarity.csv. Also print progress. No external deps beyond stdlib (numpy might not be installed? Check. pandas? Check). Let me check the availability of numpy/pandas."}}
