{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"reasoning","text":"numpy and pandas are available. Now let's write the main analysis script. First create a scratch version, iterate on it, then polish it into output/analysis.py.\n\nKey components:\n1. Parse chrom.sizes → piece offsets per arm.\n2. Parse GTF → genes. Build symbol index including plain tokens.\n3. Marker sets (curated human symbols).\n4. Symbol resolution: for each marker symbol, collect candidate genes:\n   - prefer tokens where the symbol appears with any tag or plain, exact match\n   - exclude genes on unplaced contigs (chrom not in arm list)\n   - if a symbol has >6 arm hits, mark it ambiguous → exclude\n   - if 0 hits, missing\n5. Target windows: TSS ±100kb (for the flanking region), promoter TSS ±2kb.\n6. Stream the TSV once: for each row (piece, start, end, counts[15]):\n   - arm = piece name up to the last '_' (e.g., chr1p_1 → chr1p; note chr9p has no underscore suffix! \"chr9p\" appears in sizes without a suffix; chr10q, chr11p, chr12p, chr13p, chr13q, chr14p, chr14q are also single-piece). So arm extraction: match piece against the known piece list; pre-build piece→(arm, offset).\n   - accumulate column totals.\n   - if arm has targets: for each overlapping target interval, add counts.\n   Efficient overlap search: for each arm, keep targets sorted by window start; use a bisect on window starts; check candidate targets within [pos-200000, pos+10000]. The number of targets per arm is small (<200), so a linear scan is also fine: 2.43M rows × small number of targets per arm. Total targets across all arms ~200-400. If we scan all targets of the relevant arm for each row… rows are ~2.4M; average targets per arm ~10-20 → 30-50M comparisons, might take ~30-60s in Python. With bisect, we can reduce to nearly O(1) per row. Use bisect on sorted window starts.\n7. Scoring:\n   - lib_s = column totals; CPM factor.\n   - for each gene: promoter CPM p_gs; flanking per-bin CPM; fold = p / (flank_per