s).\nIf these hold → swap_detected=true. Otherwise false with an explanation.\n\nAlso compute an independent sanity check via single-gene marker specificity: for the top candidate pair, show key genes.\n\nOK, let's write the script. First write scratch/run_analysis.py, run it, inspect results, then finalize as output/analysis.py.\n\nDetails on marker set curation: define markers as a dictionary. Also add an \"extended\" candidate list; auto-resolution picks up whatever is found.\n\nAnother important point: some symbols appear in the GTF as read-throughs, e.g. 'INS-IGF2 [hs]' — tokenized as 'INS-IGF2', not 'INS', so it won't match exact 'INS'. Fine.\n\nAlso 'UMOD' has 24 hits — excluded by the ambiguity cap. 'MUC5AC' 15 hits, 'CHIA' 8 hits, 'SCN2A' 9 hits, 'KRT8' 5 hits, 'SLC12A2' 6 hits, 'SLC26A6' 4 hits, 'ACTC1' 5 hits, 'KRT19' 4 hits… Hmm, KRT8 has 5 hits (keratin gene family). Cap at ≤6, but log which ones are used.\n\nWait, actually let me reconsider: excluding genes may lose real signal, but including too many wrong ones (e.g. 24 'UMOD' hits, many probably spurious best-hits to non-orthologous genes) adds noise. A ≤6 cap is reasonable. For keratins, family expansion is real in axolotl, but only some are true orthologs; still, if KRT8-family genes are all epithelial, including 5 hits is fine (they'll all show epithelial accessibility).\n\nPotential subtlety: z-scoring per gene across 15 samples handles absolute level differences. OK.\n\nNow let's write the script.\n\nOn streaming efficiency: parse each row with line.split('\\t'). 2.4M rows → probably 10-20s. No problem.\n\nPiece→arm mapping: parse chrom.sizes in order; group pieces by arm name: arm = re.sub(r'_\\d+$', '', piece). Pieces of an arm appear consecutively (chr1p_1, chr1p_2, chr1p_3, chr1q_1…). Accumulate offsets.\n\nGTF genes: TSS = start if strand '+', else end (1-based). Use window [TSS-2000, TSS+2000] in arm coordinates, convert to piece-local at query time? Or: at build time, convert gene's arm coordinates to (piece, local_pos) for each gene: find the piece whose [offset, offset+len) contains tss. Then promoter window may straddle piece boundaries; handle by clipping the window to the piece and possibly generating two intervals (one per piece). Rows are keyed by piece and piece-local coordinates, so build targets per piece.\n\nImplementation:\n- pieces: ordered list of (piece_name, arm, offset, length). piece_start_by_name dict.\n- for each gene on an arm: tss (1-based, arm coordinates) → 0-based arm pos = tss-1? hmm. GTF start/end are 1-based inclusive. TSS position (1-based) = start if '+' else end. Convert to 0-based half-open convention: tss0 = tss1 - 1. Bins: [piece_offset + row.start, piece_offset + row.end) in 0-based arm coordinates. Promoter window: [tss0 - 2000, tss0 + 2000) 0-based arm. Overlap with a bin if bin_start < win_end and bin_end > win_start.\n- flank window: [tss0 - 100000, tss0 + 100000).\n- clip each window to arm [0, arm_len) and split across pieces: for pieces overlapping the window, create target entries: (piece_name, w_start_local, w_end_local, gene_idx, kind), where kind ∈ {'prom','flank'}… actually simpler: create two target records per gene (promoter, flank), clip each to pieces.\n\nDuring streaming: for the current piece, keep targets sorted by w_start_local. Maintain a per-piece dict: list of (w_start_local, gene_idx, kind, w_end_local). For each row with local start s: use bisect to find targets with w_start_local <= row_end and check w_end_local > row_start. Since target count is small, collect candidates via bisect_left on (w_start - something): sort by w_start; candidates with w_start <= s+10000 (bins are ≤10kb wide, so w_start > s+10000 cannot overlap). idx = bisect_right(starts, s+10000); scan targets[:idx] in reverse and check overlap and w_end > s; stop once w_end <= … targets aren't sorted by end, so just scan all candidates with w_start <= s+10000 — could be many if windows are large (200kb flank). Per-arm targets ~dozens; worst-case scan per row ~50 → 2.4M×50 = 120M ops, too slow in Python (~2 min). Hmm.\n\nOptimization: bucketize targets by local position / 10kb bin index: piece → dict bin_idx → list of targets. Row's bin idx = s//10000. Candidate targets = targets in buckets {bin_idx - (200kb/10k=20) ... bin_idx}? Flank window 200kb = 20 bins. So check buckets bin_idx-20..bin_idx (targets starting within those buckets could still overlap row if w_end > s). Each bucket has few targets (sparse), so per-row work is ~number of overlapping targets (small) + 21 dict lookups (~2.4M × 21 = 50M dict lookups, also somewhat slow ~30-60s).\n\nBetter: interval sweep: rows are in ascending start order within a piece. Maintain an active target set: targets with w_start <= current row_end are added (via pointer), targets with w_end <= row_start are removed. Active targets all potentially overlap each row; check each active target's w_end > row_start (guaranteed if not removed) and w_start < row_end (guaranteed by pointer). So per row, iterate active targets (usually small: windows overlapping the current position). For genes with 200kb flank windows, active count = targets covering the current position — sparse, usually 0-3. Total work ≈ rows × small constant + total target events.\n\nImplementation: sort targets per piece by w_start. pointer p. active = list of (w_end, gene_idx, kind). For each row (s, e):\n - while p < len and targets[p].w_start < e: push to active; p++\n - remove entries with w_end <= s (rebuild active list occasionally)\n - for each (w_end, gidx, kind) in active: add counts.\nRemoval: active = [t for t in active if t.w_end > s] — rebuild only if needed (e.g. always; active is small).\n\nThis is efficient: ~2.4M rows × (small active set). Should run in ~15-30s total.\n\nCounts per (gene, kind, sample): arrays prom[g][15], flank[g][15], flank_bins[g] (count of bins contributing to flanks; each row counted once per gene). Note: bins overlapping both promoter and flank of