{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting! There's a clear pattern:\n\nEight organs are self-consistent (Brain, Heart, Intestine, Kidney, Liver, Lung, Pancreas, Spleen): their marker sets peak in their own labeled samples, and their samples best-match their own marker sets. Strong positive z (+0.84 to +1.46).\n\nThe remaining 7 organs (Bladder, Cloaca, GallBladder, Gill, Limb, Prostate, Stomach) show weak self-consistency. But this might be because their marker sets are weak/noisy (Cloaca, Gill, Limb, Prostate, Bladder really don't have good canonical markers that transfer to axolotl).\n\nHmm, but look at the pattern among the weak ones:\n- Stomach sample: self z = -0.48 (negative!), best match = Cloaca marker set (+0.82). And the best sample for the Stomach marker set is Gill (+0.51), with labeled Stomach ranked 14th/15.\n- GallBladder sample: self z = -0.57 (negative!), ranked 14th/15 on its own markers. Best sample for GallBladder markers = Cloaca (+0.77).\n- Cloaca sample: best matches Bladder markers (+0.96). Best sample for Cloaca markers = Stomach (+0.82).\n- Bladder markers: best sample = Cloaca (+0.96); labeled Bladder rank 6, self z +0.10.\n- Gill markers: best sample = Cloaca (+0.38); Gill sample best-matches Bladder markers (+0.83).\n- Prostate markers: best = Stomach (+0.70); Prostate sample best-matches GallBladder markers (+0.55).\n- Limb: labeled Limb rank 8 (z +0.19); best for Limb markers = Stomach (+0.76); Limb sample best-matches Prostate (+0.25).\n\nThis is messy — Cloaca's column looks like a \"universal epithelium\"? Cloaca sample scores high on Bladder (+0.96), GallBladder (+0.77), Gill (+0.38), Limb (+0.45), Prostate (+0.25)... This could mean either (a) Cloaca is actually a different tissue that shares epithelial programs with many of these, or (b) the Cloaca column contains a mixture / some other tissue.\n\nWait — the swapped pair hypothesis: if two labels were swapped, exactly two organs should show mutual inconsistency. Let me hypothesize the candidate pairs:\n\nCandidate A: GallBladder ↔ Stomach?\n- GallBladder sample self z = -0.57; best-matches Kidney (-0.16)?? No. GallBladder sample best-matches Kidney at -0.16 — all marker sets score low/negative on the GallBladder column. That's odd: the GallBladder column doesn't match any marker set. Hmm, GallBladder column scores: Bladder -0.98, Brain -0.91, Cloaca -0.56, GallBladder -0.57, Gill -0.62, Heart -0.88, Intestine -0.34, Kidney -0.16, Limb -1.25, Liver -0.24, Lung -1.35, Pancreas -0.42, Prostate -1.05, Spleen -1.07, Stomach -0.94. All negative. So the GallBladder column doesn't match any organ marker set at all. This suggests either the GallBladder data is genuinely weird (e.g., very different chromatin, low quality), or the GallBladder column actually contains a tissue that we don't have markers for... but all 15 tissues are labeled. Or the GallBladder column contains tissue X, and tissue X's marker set is one of the weak ones that scored negative... no, all are negative.\n\nHmm wait, that's suspicious: every marker set scores ≤ -0.16 on the GallBladder column. If the GallBladder column contained, say, true Bladder tissue, then Bladder markers should score high on it (Bladder markers have a strong peak somewhere: best = Cloaca +0.96). Bladder on GallBladder = -0.98, no.\n\nWhat if the GallBladder column is an outlier library (e.g., mostly closed chromatin / low signal)? Column total is 566M, middle of the pack. Hmm.\n\nCandidate B: Stomach ↔ Gill?\n- Best sample for Stomach markers = Gill (+0.51). Best match for Gill sample = Bladder (+0.83)? Not Stomach. Best match for Stomach sample = Cloaca (+0.82). Doesn't close cleanly.\n\nCandidate C: Cloaca ↔ Stomach?\n- Best sample for Stomach markers = Cloaca? No — best for Stomach markers = Gill (+0.51), Stomach column ranks 14th with -0.48. Best sample for Cloaca markers = Stomach (+0.82). So Cloaca markers prefer the Stomach column; Stomach markers prefer the Gill column. Not mutual.\n\nHmm. Let me think about the weak organs. Bladder, Cloaca, GallBladder, Gill, Limb, Prostate, Stomach are the ones with self-consistency problems. But only one pair is swapped. So at most two of these seven should be explained by the swap; the other five should show weak coherence because marker sets are poor.\n\nKey signal: which two columns show strongly positive evidence of being the other's tissue (high z on the other's markers) and strongly negative self-evidence?\n\nLooking at the sample→best-marker-set mapping:\n- Cloaca column → Bladder markers +0.96 (strong)\n- Gill column → Bladder markers +0.83 (strong)\n- Stomach column → Cloaca markers +0.82 (strong)\n- Prostate column → GallBladder markers +0.55 (moderate)\n- Bladder column → Limb markers +0.45 (moderate)\n\nAnd organ-marker → best-sample:\n- Bladder markers → Cloaca column +0.96\n- GallBladder markers → Cloaca column +0.77\n- Stomach markers → Gill column +0.51\n- Cloaca markers → Stomach column +0.82\n- Limb markers → Stomach column +0.76\n- Prostate markers → Stomach column +0.70\n- Gill markers → Cloaca column +0.38\n\nHmm, the Cloaca column looks like a bladder-like tissue (Bladder markers peak there +0.96, GallBladder markers also +0.77). The Gill column also looks bladder-like (Bladder markers +0.83). The Stomach column looks cloaca-like (Cloaca markers +0.82), and additionally Limb markers (+0.76), Prostate markers (+0.70) peak there too.\n\nIf the swap is Bladder ↔ Cloaca:\n- Bladder column should contain Cloaca tissue: Cloaca markers on Bladder column = 0.00. Not high. ✗\n\nIf the swap is Gill ↔ Bladder:\n- Bladder markers on Gill column +0.83 ✓; Gill markers on Bladder column = -0.13 ✗.\n\nIf the swap is Stomach ↔ Gill:\n- Stomach markers on Gill column +0.51 ✓; Gill markers on Stomach column +0.51 ✓!!\nWait: Gill markers on Stomach column = +0.51 (row Gill, column Stomach = 0.51). And Stomach markers on Gill column = +0.31? Let me re-check: row Stomach: Gill column = 0.51. Row Gill: Stomach column = 0.31. Hmm:\n\nRow Gill: Bladder -0.13, Brain 0.03, Cloaca 0.38, GallBladder -0.62, Gill 0.25, Heart 0.01, Intestine 0.12, Kidney -0.10, Limb -0.26, Liver -0.07, Lung 0.33, Pancreas 0.17, Prostate 0.11, Spleen -0.02, Stomach 0.31.\nSo Gill markers on Stomach column = +0.31.\nRow Stomach: Gill column = 0.51.\n\ne(Stomach→Gill) = Stomach markers on Gill column (+0.51) − Stomach markers on Stomach column (−0.48) = +0.99.\ne(Gill→Stomach) = Gill markers on Stomach column (+0.31) − Gill markers on Gill column (+0.25) = +0.06.\n\nHmm, asymmetric. e(Gill→Stomach) is weak. So under the swap Stomach↔Gill, the Stomach column (containing Gill tissue) should score highest on Gill markers — but the Stomach column scores +0.82 on Cloaca markers, higher than +0.31 on Gill markers. And the Gill column scores +0.83 on Bladder markers, higher than +0.51 on Stomach markers.\n\nThis pattern — the Cloaca column matching Bladder markers, the Gill column matching Bladder markers, the Stomach column matching Cloaca markers — looks more like a cyclic shift?! Like Bladder→Cloaca→Stomach→Gill... no wait. Let me think about the possibilities of a permutation among {Bladder, Cloaca, Gill, Stomach, GallBladder, Prostate, Limb}.\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider. It might not be a swap; it might be that these weak marker sets just share a generic epithelial/mesenchymal program. For example, Bladder markers (KRT8/18, UPK3B, SLC26A6) are generic epithelial markers → any epithelial tissue (Cloaca, Gill epithelium) scores high. Cloaca markers (CDX2, HOXC13, WNT5A, TP63) are posterior/epithelial → Stomach is also endodermal epithelium. Limb markers include HAND2 (also in Heart!), PBX1 (ubiquitous), MEIS1... TBX5 is a heart marker! I included TBX5 in the Limb set (forelimb), but TBX5 is also a cardiac TF — that's why the Limb markers score +0.41 on the Heart column. Hmm, my marker sets have some cross-contamination.\n\nSo the weak coherence of these seven organs might be an artifact of poor marker specificity, not evidence of a swap. The organs with truly strong signals (Brain, Heart, Intestine, Kidney, Liver, Lung, Pancreas, Spleen) are all self-consistent — meaning the swap does not involve any of those eight? If the swap involved one of them (say Brain), Brain markers should peak on another column and Brain column should match another organ's markers. That's not observed: Brain column best-matches Brain markers (+1.43), and the best sample for Brain markers is Brain. Same for all eight.\n\nTherefore, if a swap exists, it must be among {Bladder, Cloaca, GallBladder, Gill, Limb, Prostate, Stomach}. For those, my marker-based evidence is noisy. I need stronger evidence.\n\nHmm, wait. But let me also double-check the possibility that the swap is between two of the \"consistent eight\" — no, that's impossible: if the Brain and Heart labels were swapped, Brain markers would peak on the Heart column. Not observed.\n\nOK so the swap must be among the seven weak ones. Let me get better evidence:\n\n1. Improve marker sets for these organs (more specific markers; remove cross-contaminating ones like TBX5 from Limb, SLC26A6 shared across Bladder/GallBladder/Gill/Intestine).\n\n2. Use gene body + promoter signal for marker genes that are highly expressed.\n\n3. Use an orthogonal, unsupervised approach: correlation structure with the consistent eight. For example, if Gill and Bladder labels are swapped... hmm.\n\n4. Key insight: among the seven weak organs, exactly two should be mutually inconsistent and all others self-consistent (with respect to improved markers). The current confusion might be due to marker set quality. Let me refine:\n\nBladder markers: uroplakins UPK1A/1B/2/3A/3B are the most specific. UPK3B was found (2 hits). UPK1A/1B/2/3A were not found as exact tokens — let me search more loosely for 'UPK'. KRT20 was not found. PPARG was not found. Hmm.\n\nGill: more specific markers: ion transporters are shared with kidney (SLC12A, CA2, ATP1A1). Gill-specific vs kidney: RHCG/RHBG (Rh glycoproteins, ammonia transport — gill-specific in fish/amphibians). RHBG found (1 hit), RHCG not found.\n\nStomach: GHRL not found, PGA3/PGC found, ATP4A/B not found, MUC5AC excluded (ambiguous), GKN1 excluded (7 hits), TFF2 found, HDC found, CHIA excluded. Hmm, weak.\n\nProstate: NKX3-1 found!, HOXB13 found, TMPRSS2 found, MSMB found, KLK1 found. Androgen-regulated secretory genes.\n\nCloaca: CDX2 (shared with Intestine), HOXC13, WNT5A, TP63, HOXC10, GATA3, MSX2.\n\nLimb: HOXA13, HOXD10/11/13, MEIS1, MSX2, PBX1, TBX5 (remove — cardiac), HAND2 (shared with heart), PITX1 missing, GREM1 missing, SHH missing.\n\nGallBladder: KRT8/18/19, EPCAM, ANXA4 (missing), SLC26A6, ONECUT1 (shared with liver/pancreas), HNF1B (shared with kidney/pancreas), CFTR, SOX9 missing, MUC1 missing.\n\nHonestly, the cleanest signal is this: GallBladder column matches no marker set (all ≤ −0.16). That's a strong anomaly. And GallBladder markers peak on the Cloaca column (+0.77), with labeled GallBladder ranking 14th.\n\nLet me compute the pairwise swap score matrix to see the ranking. But I should also refine the markers first. Actually, let me compute the swap score now with the current scores to see the candidates, then refine.\n\nAnother consideration: maybe I should reconsider the metric. The consensus score z-normalizes per gene across samples. For organs with weak marker sets, the z just amplifies noise. But the relative pattern still contains information.\n\nLet me compute e(A→B) = score(A markers on B column) − score(A markers on A column), and swap_score = e(A→B) + e(B→A).\n\nAlso, let me output the score matrix on the \"fold\" metric and on the \"cpm\" metric separately, to see if they agree.\n\nAnd let me also compute an alternative evidence: correlation between each pair of columns... actually here's a cleaner orthogonal approach for the weak organs: pairwise correlation of genome-wide profiles between the two suspected columns and the remaining columns can't identify the swap.\n\nAlternative: use marker genes that distinguish within the confusion set. For example, does axolotl stomach express pepsinogen (PGA3/PGC)? Stomach markers on Stomach column = −0.48, which is bad. If the Stomach column really contained stomach tissue, PGA3/PGC promoters should be open. Let me look at individual gene values for the key hallmark genes on the key columns — inspect gene-level z for INS, ALB, TNNT2, VIL1, SFTPC, SNAP25, SLC12A1, HBA1 (already consistent), and for the weak organs: UPK3B (Bladder), RHBG (Gill), PGA3/PGC/TFF2/HDC (Stomach), NKX3-1/HOXB13/TMPRSS2/MSMB (Prostate), CDX2/HOXC13/TP63 (Cloaca), HOXA13/HOXD13 (Limb), KRT19/EPCAM/CFTR/SLC26A6/ONECUT1 (GallBladder).\n\nLet me output the gene-level z matrix (rows=genes, columns=samples) for these weak-organ genes. That'll tell me exactly which columns carry which programs."}}
