{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"reasoning","text":"Okay, let me look at the Gill gene rows. The most gill-specific genes: RHBG and RHCG (Rh glycoproteins — ammonia transporters, enriched in gill ionocytes):\n- RHBG:6841: Gill column +2.11 (highest), Bladder column +1.27, Stomach +1.44.\n- RHCG:4168: Prostate column +2.45 (!), Bladder +1.53, Gill +1.38.\n\nHmm, interesting. RHBG is highest in the Gill column, but RHCG is highest in the Prostate column. The Gill column has both open (+2.11, +1.38). The Bladder column also has both open (+1.27, +1.53).\n\nIonocyte genes in the Gill column: RHBG +2.11, RHCG +1.38, SLC12A2:2921 +1.92, AQP3:1604 +1.10, SLC12A2:4607 0.01... mixed.\n\nBladder column: UPK1A +0.79, UPK1B +1.33, UPK3B:4459 +0.90, RHBG +1.27, RHCG +1.53, SLC12A2:4607 +1.49. So the Bladder column has a mixed urothelial + \"gill-like\" (RHBG/RHCG) profile.\n\nHmm, wait, this is getting confusing. Let me think about what RHBG/RHCG expression means. In fish/amphibians, Rhcg/Rhbg are expressed in gill ionocytes, and also in kidney and skin. So they're not absolutely gill-specific.\n\nLet me take yet another approach: rather than relying on my (possibly noisy) curated marker sets, let me think about which single pair swap best explains the global pattern. The swap-score ranking is as follows:\n\n1. Prostate ↔ Stomach +1.414 (e: +0.66/+0.76 — both directions positive!)\n2. Cloaca ↔ Stomach +1.310 (+0.69/+0.62 both positive)\n3. Bladder ↔ Stomach +1.175 (+0.51/+0.66 both positive)\n4. Limb ↔ Stomach +1.091 (+0.56/+0.53 both positive)\n5. Gill ↔ Stomach +1.051 (+0.06/+0.99)\n\nStomach is involved in all top 5 pairs. This suggests the Stomach column is anomalous (doesn't match stomach markers; self z −0.48), and something matches stomach markers poorly... wait, which column best matches stomach markers? The Gill column (+0.51). And what does the Stomach column match? Cloaca markers (+0.82)...\n\nHmm, but wait: is the Stomach marker set any good? Stomach markers resolved to: PGA3, PGC, ATP4B, HDC ×2, MUC1, TFF2 ×2 (MUC5AC, GKN1, CHIA excluded as ambiguous). Only 6 symbols. TFF2 is −2.2 on the Stomach column — TFF2 is a stomach-specific trefoil factor; its promoter should be open in stomach! Unless the axolotl TFF2 annotation is wrong (paralog misassignment). Also, in the Brain column TFF2 is +0.44/+0.07, Heart +1.65/+1.12... strange. TFF2 being open in Heart? Suspicious annotation.\n\nMSMB:1701 (prostate marker) is +2.95 on the Stomach column. HOXB13 is +1.35 on the Stomach column. HOXB13 is a prostate lineage marker. Furthermore, KRT18, UPK1B, UPK3B are open in the Stomach column. The Stomach column looks like urogenital epithelium (prostate/urothelial), not stomach.\n\nAnd the Prostate column: matches GallBladder markers (+0.55), Prostate self z +0.04. What is the Prostate column? CA2 +2.62 (carbonic anhydrase!), SLC4A2 +3.07 (!!), RHCG +2.45, TMPRSS2:7139? No wait, that's −1.00... SLC9A3 +1.32, ATP1B1 +1.38, STEAP4:2189? −0.08... CA2 +2.62 + SLC4A2 +3.07 + RHCG +2.45 — this is an ion-transporting epithelial profile. Gill ionocytes express CA2, SLC4A2 (band 3/anion exchanger in erythrocytes/ionocytes), RHCG. Hmm! Actually, in fish gills, CA2 and SLC4A2 (SLC4A2 is in erythrocytes, but also in some ionocytes?), RHCG is definitely in gill ionocytes.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The Prostate column might actually be GILL tissue! CA2 +2.62, RHCG +2.45, SLC4A2 +3.07, SLC9A3 +1.32, AQP3:7092? −0.39... And the Gill column: CA2 +0.80, RHCG +1.38, SLC4A2 −0.19, SLC9A3 +0.74.\n\nHmm, but the Prostate column also has: MUC1 +1.76, KRT8:9406 +0.06... no, that's not right. Let me grab the Prostate column's values across all marker genes and see what its profile is.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider the whole picture. Top pairs all include Stomach. The Stomach column doesn't look like stomach (TFF2 −2.2, PGA/PGC ~0, MUC1 +1.76 is not stomach-specific, MSMB +2.95, HOXB13 +1.35, UPK1B +1.15, UPK3B +1.56, KRT18 +1.4). The Stomach column looks like a urogenital sinus derivative (prostate-like + urothelial-like).\n\nWhich column looks like actual stomach? Check the PGA3/PGC rows: nearly flat everywhere (max Liver +0.58/+0.67, Pancreas +0.63/+0.04). Hmm, no column shows strong pepsinogen promoter accessibility. That's odd. Maybe the axolotl PGA3/PGC annotations are partial? PGA3 is an nr-tag hit. Stomach tissue should definitely have open pepsinogen promoters... unless stomach sample chromatin is dominated by something else (muscle layer? mucosa?).\n\nHmm wait, let me actually check: are there any gastric markers open in any column? ATP4B: Lung +1.28, Spleen +1.35... ATP4B is not a good hit? Hmm.\n\nLet me check MUC5AC (excluded due to 15 hits) — maybe some hits are actually stomach-specific. And GKN1 (7 hits), CHIA (9 hits). Excluding them as ambiguous might have cost us our best stomach markers. Alternative: include all hits for these, but weight them... no. Better approach: for each hit of MUC5AC/GKN1/CHIA, look at which column it's open in and see if any hit specifically marks one column.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about the structure of this benchmark. The task is named \"ls03-atac-sample-swap\", the data probably comes from a published axolotl organ ATAC-seq atlas, with exactly two labels swapped for the benchmark. A robust method should be able to identify them.\n\nGiven that 8 organs are clearly self-consistent, the swap must be among the remaining 7: {Bladder, Cloaca, GallBladder, Gill, Limb, Prostate, Stomach}.\n\nAmong these, which pair shows a mutual best-match relationship (organ A's markers peak in column B, organ B's markers peak in column A)?\n\nFrom the \"best sample per marker set\" list:\n- Bladder markers → Cloaca column (+0.96)\n- Cloaca markers → Stomach column (+0.82)\n- GallBladder markers → Cloaca column (+0.77)\n- Gill markers → Cloaca column (+0.38)\n- Limb markers → Stomach column (+0.76)\n- Prostate markers → Stomach column (+0.70)\n- Stomach markers → Gill column (+0.51)\n\nFrom the \"best marker set per sample\" list:\n- Bladder column → Limb (+0.45)\n- Cloaca column → Bladder (+0.96)\n- GallBladder column → Kidney (−0.16) [nothing positive!]\n- Gill column → Bladder (+0.83)\n- Limb column → Prostate (+0.25)\n- Prostate column → GallBladder (+0.55)\n- Stomach column → Cloaca (+0.82)\n\nHmm. Cycles: Cloaca column ↔ Bladder markers; Gill column ↔ Bladder markers; Stomach column ↔ Cloaca markers; Prostate column ↔ GallBladder markers; GallBladder column → nothing; Limb column ↔ Prostate markers; Bladder column ↔ Limb markers.\n\nInteresting: Bladder column → Limb markers (+0.45), Limb column → Prostate markers (+0.25), Prostate column → GallBladder markers (+0.55), GallBladder column → Kidney (−0.16)... looks like a chain of misassignments. If this were a cyclic permutation, multiple labels would have been shuffled, but the task says exactly two.\n\nHowever, these weak-organ marker sets are noisy. Let me focus on the strongest individual anomalies:\n\n1. UPK1A/UPK3B (urothelium-specific) are most open in the Gill column (+2.69, +2.14). This is the strongest single cross-organ signal.\n2. MSMB:1701 in the Stomach column +2.95, HOXB13 in Stomach column +1.35 (prostate markers).\n3. SLC4A2 in the Prostate column +3.07, CA2 +2.62, RHCG +2.45 (ion-transport/gill-like).\n4. GallBladder column: negative for everything.\n\nHmm, what if two swaps... no, only one pair.\n\nLet me hypothesize Stomach ↔ Prostate (top swap score +1.414, both directions positive):\n- Stomach column = prostate tissue: MSMB ✓ (strong), HOXB13 ✓, UPK1B/UPK3B ✓ (prostatic urethra is urothelial!), KRT18 ✓ (prostatic epithelium is KRT8/18+), MUC1 ✓, PPARG +1.37?? Hmm. But TMPRSS2:7139 in Stomach column −1.31, STEAP4 −0.08/−0.31, NKX3-1 +0.56, KLK? AR +0.67. Mixed.\n- Prostate column = stomach tissue: Stomach markers on Prostate column +0.27 (weakly positive). Individual: PGA3 +0.14, PGC +0.34, ATP4B +0.45, MUC1 +1.76, TFF2 −0.37, HDC −1.05/+0.55. But CA2 +2.62, SLC4A2 +3.07, RHCG +2.45 in the Prostate column — not stomach. Stomach doesn't strongly express CA2/SLC4A2/RHCG (parietal cells express ATP4A/B and CA2 for acid secretion! CA2 is in parietal cells! CA2 +2.62 in Prostate column could be parietal cell signal! But ATP4B there is only +0.45, weak...).\n\nHmm. Now hypothesize Gill ↔ Bladder (+0.344, directions +0.72/−0.38 — asymmetric, reverse direction negative):\n- Gill column = bladder: UPK1A +2.69 ✓✓, UPK3B +2.14 ✓✓, UPK1B +0.96 ✓, RHBG +2.11, RHCG +1.38 (Rh proteins are also in urothelium? Actually Rhcg is expressed in kidney/urinary tract in mammals!). SLC12A2:2921 +1.92. Very consistent with urothelium!\n- Bladder column = gill: Gill markers on Bladder column −0.38 overall, but RHBG +1.27 ✓, RHCG +1.53 ✓, SLC12A2:4607 +1.49 ✓, SLC9A3 −0.26, CA2 +0.22, AQP3 +0.07... partial gill-like ionocyte signal. Gill set z on Bladder column is negative because ATP1A1 genes are negative there (ATP1A1:6820 Bladder column −1.69, 6821 −0.26, 6825 −1.14, 6884 −0.26, ATP1B1 −0.71, ATP6V1A +1.10). ATP1A1 is a Na/K ATPase alpha1 — ubiquitous; its paralog z-scores are noisy. The \"gill\" signal from ATP1A1 is weak evidence anyway.\n\nHmm, so Gill ↔ Bladder: strong evidence that Gill column is bladder (UPK!), partial evidence that Bladder column is gill (RHBG/RHCG/SLC12A2 open).\n\nNext, hypothesize Stomach ↔ Prostate: evidence is MSMB/HOXB13 in Stomach column, weak stomach signal in Prostate column.\n\nAnd Cloaca ↔ Stomach (+1.310): Stomach column matches Cloaca markers +0.82 (CDX2? CDX2 in Stomach column +2.46!, HOXC11 +2.02, HOXC12 +1.35, WNT5A +0.36...). Hmm wait, CDX2 +2.46 in Stomach column! CDX2 is an intestinal/homeobox TF of the caudal endoderm. But Intestine column also has CDX2 +2.37, and Intestine is self-consistent. CDX2 is open in both Intestine and Stomach columns. In axolotl, Cdx2 is expressed in posterior endoderm (intestine); stomach is anterior endoderm (Cdx2 negative). Hmm, but wait — could the Stomach column actually be Intestine? No, the Intestine column is self-consistent (Intestine markers rank 1 there: VIL1, CDX2, FABP, SI... let me confirm: Intestine column's best match is Intestine +1.10).\n\nHmm, okay. What about the possibility that the swapped pair is Stomach ↔ Cloaca:\n- Stomach column = cloaca tissue: CDX2 +2.46 ✓ (cloaca is posterior endoderm — CDX2+!), HOXC11 +2.02 ✓, HOXC12 +1.35 ✓, KRT8 +0.83 ✓, PPARG +1.37, PLAC8 +1.39 ✓, UPK1B +1.15 ✓ (cloaca includes urothelium!), UPK3B:4459 +1.56 ✓, MUC1 +1.76 ✓. Cloaca is the common chamber receiving gut + urinary + genital ducts — its epithelium is partly intestinal (CDX2+) and partly urothelial (UPK+)! The Stomach column's profile (CDX2+, UPK+, KRT18+, MSMB+, HOXB13+) is very consistent with CLOACA!\n- Cloaca column = stomach tissue: Stomach markers on Cloaca column +0.62: PGA3 +0.07, PGC +0.69, ATP4B −1.75?, HDC −0.11/+1.30, MUC1 +1.66 ✓, TFF2 −0.60/−0.18. Hmm, mixed. And the Cloaca column has: SHH +2.52!, WNT5A +1.63, KRT8 +1.7, KRT18 +1.44, MUC1 +1.66, ONECUT1 +1.92, FGFR3 +1.26, PLAC8 +1.91, GRHL3 +0.55, UPK3A:7436 +2.65, CDX2 +0.28...\n\nHmm, the Cloaca column's SHH +2.52 and WNT5A and KRT8/KRT18... SHH is strongly expressed in stomach epithelium! Sonic hedgehog is a key morphogen in gastric gland development; SHH is highly expressed in gastric mucosa! In fact, SHH is a marker of gastric epithelium (parietal/chief cell lineage differentiation). And the stomach also has WNT signaling. The Cloaca column's SHH+2.52 could be gastric signal!\n\nHmm, this is really ambiguous between Stomach↔Cloaca and Stomach↔Prostate and Gill↔Bladder.\n\nLet me be more systematic. Cleanest approach: for each candidate pair, build an improved organ-specific marker set, and quantify bidirectional evidence with only the most specific markers (excluding shared ones). Also, add more markers for these organs by searching GTF more comprehensively.\n\nAdditional marker ideas:\n- Stomach: GHRL not found... search 'GHRL'? Not found. MUC5AC/GKN1/CHIA hits — examine individual patterns. Also 'PGA' (PGA3 found), 'GIF' intrinsic factor found? Check GIF row. 'CLIC6'? 'KCNE2'? 'KCNQ1'? Parietal cell: ATP4A/B, KCNQ1/KCNE2, CLIC6. Chief cells: PGC, PGA, LIPF, GIF. Mucous cells: MUC5AC, MUC6, TFF1/2, GKN1/2. ECL cells: HDC, CHGA, DDC. G cells: GAST. Let me check availability of CHGA, DDC, KCNQ1, KCNE2, CLIC6.\n- Gill: more specific: search 'RHBG' found, 'RHCG' found (1 hit, RHCG:4168 — wait, RHCG wasn't in the first check but now appears? Yes: RHCG:4168 appears in Gill set output — good, must have been found as a plain token). Add 'SLC26A6', 'NCC'? 'ATP6V1B'? 'ATP6V0A'? 'SLC4A1' found. 'CA15'? 'CA14'? Fish/amphibian gills also have 'SLC26A6', 'SLC26A3', pendrin (SLC26A4). Let me check SLC26A4.\n- Bladder/urothelium: more UPK hits? Search 'UPK' in GTF. Also 'KRT20', 'PPARG', 'ELF3', 'GRHL3' (found), 'PLAC8' (found), 'SLC26A6'. Urothelial plaque: UPK1A/1B/2/3A/3B. Search 'UPII', 'UPK'.\n- Prostate: search 'KLK' family (KLK1 found on contig — not usable), 'ACPP'? Not found. 'PAP'? 'AZGP1'? Found? Not in output (only TMPRSS2, MSMB, STEAP4, KLK1?, NKX3-1, HOXB13, SLC45A3). Prostate set resolves to 7 symbols/11 genes. Hmm.\n\nAlso — importantly — I should reconsider using gene body signal. Axolotl genes are huge; gene body ATAC can add information. But promoter signal is the main one.\n\nAlternative approach — use all genes, unsupervised: compute per-sample genome-wide \"organ identity\" using top variable bins and cluster the 15 samples. The two swapped samples should cluster... no wait. Swapping labels doesn't change the data; clustering just shows the relationships among the 15 profiles. We can't detect swaps without external annotation. But if we have marker-based annotations for each of the 15 organs, we can assign each column to its most likely organ, and find collisions (two columns assigned to the same organ) or mismatches. The problem is that organs with weak markers are hard to annotate.\n\nLet me try a comprehensive assignment approach:\n1. For each organ, compute the sample that maximizes the organ's marker score (argmax over columns).\n2. If exactly one pair is swapped, then for the swapped organs A, B: argmax(M_A) = B column, argmax(M_B) = A column. For the other 13 organs, argmax(M_O) = O column.\n\nCurrently: argmax: Bladder→Cloaca, Brain→Brain, Cloaca→Stomach, GallBladder→Cloaca, Gill→Cloaca, Heart→Heart, Intestine→Intestine, Kidney→Kidney, Limb→Stomach, Liver→Liver, Lung→Lung, Pancreas→Pancreas, Prostate→Stomach, Spleen→Spleen, Stomach→Gill.\n\nOnly the 8 strong organs match. The 7 weak ones point to: Cloaca column (×3: Bladder, GallBladder, Gill markers), Stomach column (×3: Cloaca, Limb, Prostate markers), Gill column (Stomach markers).\n\nIf exactly two labels were swapped, then for 13 organs argmax(M_O) = own column, and for the swapped pair argmax(M_A) = B, argmax(M_B) = A. But we see 7 organs displaced. So either the marker sets for these 7 are too weak to trust argmax, or... hmm.\n\nBut wait — if the swap is Stomach ↔ Cloaca: then Cloaca markers should have argmax = Stomach column ✓ (yes, +0.82), and Stomach markers should have argmax = Cloaca column ✗ (argmax is Gill column +0.51, Cloaca column +0.14?). Hmm, row Stomach: Cloaca column +0.14. No.\n\nIf the swap is Stomach ↔ Gill: Stomach markers argmax = Gill ✓ (+0.51); Gill markers argmax = Stomach ✗ (argmax is Cloaca +0.38; Stomach column +0.31 — close second!). Hmm, Gill markers: Cloaca +0.38 vs Stomach +0.31 — within noise range.\n\nIf the swap is Gill ↔ Bladder: Bladder markers argmax = Gill? ✗ (argmax is Cloaca +0.96; Gill column +0.83 second). Gill markers argmax = Bladder? ✗ (argmax is Cloaca +0.38; Bladder −0.13). No.\n\nHmm, none fit perfectly. The problem: the Cloaca column scores high on many marker sets (Bladder +0.96, GallBladder +0.77, Gill +0.38, Limb +0.45). And the Stomach column also scores high on many (Cloaca +0.82, Limb +0.76, Prostate +0.70, Bladder +0.62).\n\nWhat if the Cloaca and Stomach columns are both correctly labeled, and their \"promiscuous\" scores just reflect that these marker sets contain generic epithelial/posterior genes? And the real swap is elsewhere, like GallBladder ↔ something? The GallBladder column matches nothing (all z ≤ −0.16). This is a big anomaly: a real tissue should match some marker set. Unless the GallBladder column's data is actually an organ whose marker set is globally weak... all 15 organs are in our list. If the GallBladder column contained tissue X, then X's marker set should score positively there. All 15 score negative. So either the GallBladder column is a technical outlier, or all our marker sets fail on it.\n\nHmm wait — what if the GallBladder column has low library complexity, and CPM normalization can't fix it? Let me check the fraction of promoter bins with zero counts for the GallBladder column.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: z-scoring per gene across samples forces some samples to be negative for each gene. For a sample to be negative across all marker sets, it must consistently have below-average accessibility at marker promoters. That could happen if the sample's chromatin is globally depleted at promoter regions... or if the sample is truly an outlier organ we can't annotate.\n\nLet me check some global statistics per column: fraction of nonzero bins, total counts in promoter bins (fraction of reads at marker promoters), GC? Also check correlation of columns at genome-wide bins.\n\nActually, here's another thought — the GallBladder column's total library size is 566M (middle of the pack). Let me compute the correlation of columns at genome-wide bins. If GallBladder column correlates poorly with everything, it's an outlier tissue.\n\nAlso! Another idea: use known organ relationships to find a tissue the GallBladder column resembles. GallBladder column's highest (least negative) scores: Kidney −0.16, GallBladder −0.57, Stomach −0.94... all negative. Hmm.\n\nLet me compute a genome