{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting observations from the genome-wide correlations:\n\n1. The Heart row/column is a clear outlier (correlation ~0.47-0.65 with everything), and moreover the Heart sample has only 8% all-zero bins, whereas the others have >99.9%! That is, the Heart column has signal almost everywhere (including intergenic regions?) — weird. Wait, \"fraction of bins that are all-zero per sample\" = fraction of bins where that sample's value is 0. Heart: 0.918 → 8.2% of bins have non-zero signal, while other samples have <0.05% non-zero bins. This is a huge difference. Hmm! The Heart library is far more broadly covered — is this because Heart has high background in this assay, or because Heart's data came from a different pipeline? Interesting but wait — could this be a hint about the swap? If Heart's label were swapped with another organ, that other organ's column should have this broad-coverage property... but Heart is self-consistent with cardiac markers (MYH6/7, TNNT2, etc. +0.84). So the Heart column contains cardiac chromatin with broad coverage. Hmm, maybe heart tissue just has very open chromatin globally? 8% of the 32Gb genome being accessible seems excessive. Actually wait — the \"all-zero bins\" statistic: bins with zero counts across all samples are likely excluded from the file (rows are omitted). Rows exist if at least one sample is non-zero. Heart being non-zero in 8.2% of the existing bins... hmm, no wait: X is the loaded matrix (2.43M rows), and nz = fraction of rows with X[:,j] > 0. For Heart, 8.2% of rows are non-zero; for others, about 0.01-0.04%. So the Heart sample has reads in about 200,000 bins, while the others have reads in about 250-1000 bins??\n\nWait, that can't be right: Brain library = 1.17 billion counts spread over ~700 bins? Average ~1.6M per bin?? Let me check: fraction of Brain non-zero bins = 1.0000?? Does that mean Brain is non-zero in 100% of rows?? Oh wait, look: Brain's nz = 1.0000, and most samples are 0.9996-1.0000. Only Heart is 0.918. I was misreading — the values are ~1.0, not ~0.0001! \"Fraction of bins that are all-zero per sample\" was outputting (X>0).mean = fraction non-zero. So Brain is non-zero in 100% of bins; Heart in 91.8% of bins. OK, that's more reasonable: essentially every sample has signal in every retained bin. The Heart column is slightly more sparse (8% of retained bins are zero). Not a QC red flag.\n\nHmm, OK wait, but that means the file retains almost every bin. 2.43M bins × 10kb = 24.3Gb — roughly matches the sum of the chromosome sizes. Good.\n\n2. Correlation structure: everything correlates highly with everything (0.7-0.93), which is typical for ATAC binning over 10kb across organs (dominated by common open chromatin). Notable high correlations:\n- Bladder–Limb 0.931 (highest off-diagonal!), Bladder–Kidney 0.914, Bladder–Lung 0.907, Limb–Kidney 0.919, Gill–Lung 0.923, Gill–Limb 0.907, Lung–Kidney 0.878, Limb–Spleen 0.875, Limb–Prostate 0.870, Gill–Prostate 0.862, Gill–Brain 0.843...\n- GallBladder is the most isolated sample: highest correlation is Pancreas 0.861, then Liver 0.780; lowest correlations with others (Prostate 0.647, Spleen 0.641, Heart 0.470, Cloaca 0.708, Gill 0.709, Bladder 0.733, Lung 0.725, Intestine/Stomach 0.770/0.713).\n\nGallBladder being isolated in correlation space is consistent with it matching no marker set. Hmm, but if the GallBladder column really contained gallbladder tissue, it should correlate most with Liver (biliary lineage) — 0.780 is only moderate; and it should also correlate with Pancreas (0.861?). GallBladder–Pancreas 0.861 > GallBladder–Liver 0.780. Hmm.\n\nWhat if the GallBladder column were actually Pancreas tissue? No — the Pancreas column is self-consistent (INS, GCG, PDX1 +0.85 rank 1).\n\nWhat if the GallBladder column contained Limb tissue? Limb column is self... Limb self z +0.19, rank 8/15 — weak, but Limb markers peak in the Stomach column (+0.76